{"name":"Fruitless Fig Tree","hex":"#448822","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fruitless","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bearing no fruit; barren.","Unproductive, useless."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fruitless"},{"term":"fig","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The fruit of the fig tree, pear-shaped and containing many small seeds. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A fruit-bearing tree or shrub of the genus Ficus that is native mainly to the tropics."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To insult with a fico, a contemptuous gesture.","To put into the head of, as something useless or contemptible."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fig"},{"term":"tree","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A perennial woody plant taller and larger than a shrub with a wooden trunk and, at some distance from the ground, having leaves and branches.","Any other plant (such as a large shrub or herb) that is reminiscent of the above in form and size."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To chase (an animal or person) up a tree.","To place in a tree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tree"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ficus","blocks":[{"text":"Ficus is a genus of about 850 species of woody trees, shrubs, vines, epiphytes and hemiepiphytes in the family Moraceae. Collectively known as fig trees or figs, they are native throughout the tropics with a few species extending into the semi-warm temperate zone. Many Ficus species are grown for their fruits, though only two species, the common fig and sycamore fig, are cultivated to any extent, with common fig being the type species and by far the most important. The fruit of most other species are also edible though they are usually of only local economic importance or eaten as bushfood. However, they are extremely important food resources for wildlife. Figs are also of considerable cultural importance throughout the tropics, both as objects of worship and for their many practical uses."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Sycomoros_old.jpg/330px-Sycomoros_old.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ficus","wikidata":"Q59798"},{"title":"Fruitless (gene)","blocks":[{"text":"The fruitless gene (fru) is a Drosophila melanogaster gene that encodes several variants of a putative transcription factor protein. Normal fruitless function is required for proper development of several anatomical structures necessary for courtship, including motor neurons which innervate muscles needed for fly sexual behaviors. The gene does not have an obvious mammalian homolog, but appears to function in sex determination in species as distant as the mosquito Anopheles gambiae."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fruitless_(gene)","wikidata":"Q1470924"},{"title":"Fig","blocks":[{"text":"The fig is the edible fruit of Ficus carica, a species of tree or shrub in the flowering plant family Moraceae, native to the Mediterranean region, and to western and southern Asia. It has been cultivated since ancient times and is now widely grown throughout the world. Ficus carica is the type species of the genus Ficus, which comprises over 800 tropical and subtropical plant species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Ficus_carica_L%2C_1771.jpg/330px-Ficus_carica_L%2C_1771.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fig","wikidata":"Q36146"},{"title":"Tree","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a tree is a perennial plant with an elongated stem, or trunk, usually supporting branches and leaves. In some usages, the definition of a tree may be narrower, e.g., including only woody plants with secondary growth, only plants that are usable for timber, or only plants above a specified height. Wider definitions include taller palms, tree ferns, bananas, and bamboos."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Usamljeni_jasen_-_panoramio_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Usamljeni_jasen_-_panoramio_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tree","wikidata":"Q10884"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ficus-AerialRoot.jpg/500px-Ficus-AerialRoot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ficus-AerialRoot.jpg/1280px-Ficus-AerialRoot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ficus-AerialRoot.jpg/500px-Ficus-AerialRoot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aerial roots that may eventually provide structural support","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFicus-AerialRoot.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Ficus_carica_tree.jpg/500px-Ficus_carica_tree.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Ficus_carica_tree.jpg/500px-Ficus_carica_tree.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Ficus carica (common fig)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFicus_carica_tree.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/%E8%8F%A9%E6%8F%90%E6%A8%B9_Ficus_religiosa_20210421111446_01.jpg/500px-%E8%8F%A9%E6%8F%90%E6%A8%B9_Ficus_religiosa_20210421111446_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/%E8%8F%A9%E6%8F%90%E6%A8%B9_Ficus_religiosa_20210421111446_01.jpg/1280px-%E8%8F%A9%E6%8F%90%E6%A8%B9_Ficus_religiosa_20210421111446_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikiped